chosen:
Sure! I think I can help. Here’s how I’d do it.  First I’d grab a reference to the window object that refers to the actual browser window, using something like the JavaScript API:

window.location = window.location;

Next I’d write some JavaScript code to detect whenever the user clicks or uses any sort of keyboard or mouse input to cause the browser to navigate to a new URL.

window.onblur = function() {
    // Is this the current URL?  If so, do nothing
    if (window.location.href == window.location.href)
        return;

    // Otherwise, compare against the current URL
    window.location.href = window.location.href;
};

Does that make sense?

rejected:
Sure, there are many different ways to do that, but one common method is to add the listener event on any <a> element, like

document.getElementById("aElementId").addEventListener("click", onClick, false);

and then in the function (named onClick here) you can have something like

function onClick(event){
  alert(event.target.href); // Where 'event' is the event that triggered the function
}

